┃ * Stirling permutations, permutations of the multiset of numbers 1, 1, 2, 2, ..., k, k in which each pair of equal numbers is separated only by larger numbers, where *k* = ⁠*n* + 1/2⁠. The two copies of k must be adjacent; removing them from the permutation leaves a permutation in which the maximum element is *k* − 1, with n positions into which the adjacent pair of k values may be placed. From this recursive construction, a proof that the Stirling permutations are counted by the double permutations follows by induction. Alternatively, instead of the restriction that values between a pair may be larger than it, one may also consider the permutations of this multiset in which the first copies of each pair appear in sorted order; such a permutation defines a matching on the 2*k* positions of the permutation, so again the number of permutations may be counted by the double permutations.
